### What concurrency issues does HashMap cause?

A plain `HashMap` is designed for single-threaded use. It has no internal locks, no volatile fields, and no memory visibility guarantees. When multiple threads read and write a HashMap concurrently, three categories of failure can occur: structural corruption, data loss, and stale reads.

The most dramatic failure happened in Java 7, where the `resize()` method reversed the linked list in each bucket using head insertion. When two threads triggered a resize at the same time, they could create a circular reference in a bucket's linked list. Any subsequent call to `get()` that landed in that bucket would follow the `next` pointers in an infinite loop, consuming 100% CPU on that thread without ever throwing an exception.

Java 8 replaced head insertion with tail insertion, which eliminated the circular-list problem. However, concurrent writes still corrupt the internal data structure: two threads can both see a bucket as empty and both insert, causing one entry to be silently lost.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class UnsafeCacheDemo {
    // WRONG: plain HashMap shared across threads
    private static final Map<String, BigDecimal> balanceCache = new HashMap<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Runnable writer = () -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) {
                balanceCache.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal(i));
            }
        };
        Thread t1 = new Thread(writer, "Writer-1");
        Thread t2 = new Thread(writer, "Writer-2");
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t1.join();
        t2.join();
        // Result is unpredictable: lost updates, possible infinite loop on Java 7
        System.out.println("Final size: " + balanceCache.size());
    }
}
```

### Why does HashMap cause an infinite loop in Java 7?

In Java 7, the `HashMap.transfer()` method rehashes entries from the old table to the new table using head insertion. Head insertion reverses the order of entries in each bucket. When two threads resize the same HashMap simultaneously, the following sequence can occur.

Thread-1 reads that node A points to node B. Thread-2 completes the transfer first and reverses the list so that B now points to A. Thread-1 resumes and sets A's next pointer to B, but B already points back to A. The result is a circular linked list: A points to B, B points to A. Any `get()` call that hashes to this bucket follows the `next` pointers forever.

The thread appears as RUNNABLE in a thread dump, not BLOCKED or WAITING, because it is actively executing CPU instructions. No exception is thrown. The application appears alive but the thread never completes.

- Thread state is RUNNABLE, not BLOCKED, making it hard to detect.
- CPU usage spikes to 100% on that core.
- No exception is thrown and no error is logged.
- Java 8 fixed this by using tail insertion, which preserves list order during resize.

### How do you fix HashMap concurrency issues?

The primary fix is to replace `HashMap` with `ConcurrentHashMap`. It provides thread safety with fine-grained locking and lock-free reads.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class SafeCacheDemo {
    // CORRECT: ConcurrentHashMap with per-bucket synchronization
    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> balanceCache =
            new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Runnable writer = () -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) {
                balanceCache.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal(i));
            }
        };
        Thread t1 = new Thread(writer, "Writer-1");
        Thread t2 = new Thread(writer, "Writer-2");
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t1.join();
        t2.join();
        System.out.println("Final value: " + balanceCache.get("SAV2001"));
        // Always consistent, no corruption, no infinite loop
    }
}
```

### How is ConcurrentHashMap different from Collections.synchronizedMap?

`Collections.synchronizedMap()` wraps every operation with a single global lock. Only one thread can read or write at a time. `ConcurrentHashMap` uses fine-grained per-bucket locking for writes and volatile reads for gets, allowing much higher concurrency.

| Feature | ConcurrentHashMap | synchronizedMap |
|---|---|---|
| Lock granularity | Per-bucket (fine) | Entire map (coarse) |
| Read locking | Lock-free volatile read | Full lock on every read |
| Write locking | CAS or synchronized(firstNode) | Full lock on every write |
| Concurrent readers | Unlimited | One at a time |
| Atomic compound ops | computeIfAbsent, merge | None built in |
| Iterator behavior | Weakly consistent | Fail-fast (needs external sync) |
| Null keys/values | Not allowed | Allowed |

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class MapComparisonDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Option 1: synchronizedMap (simple but slow)
        Map<String, BigDecimal> syncMap =
                Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());
        syncMap.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("50000.00"));
        // Every get() and put() acquires the SAME lock

        // Option 2: ConcurrentHashMap (fast, production-grade)
        ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> concMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        concMap.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("50000.00"));
        // get() uses volatile read with zero lock overhead
        // put() locks only the affected bucket
    }
}
```

### What are the atomic compound operations on ConcurrentHashMap?

ConcurrentHashMap provides atomic compound operations that eliminate the need for external synchronization. The most important is `computeIfAbsent`, which loads a value exactly once even when many threads request the same missing key simultaneously.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class AtomicOpsDemo {
    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> cache =
            new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    static BigDecimal loadBalanceFromDB(String accountId) {
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                + " loading from DB: " + accountId);
        return new BigDecimal("75000.00");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // computeIfAbsent: atomic check-and-load
        BigDecimal balance = cache.computeIfAbsent("SAV2001",
                AtomicOpsDemo::loadBalanceFromDB);
        // Only ONE thread loads from DB, even with 100 concurrent requests

        // merge: atomic read-modify-write
        cache.merge("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("1000.00"), BigDecimal::add);
        // Atomically adds 1000 to the existing balance

        // compute: atomic update with access to old value
        cache.compute("SAV2001", (key, oldVal) ->
                oldVal == null ? BigDecimal.ZERO : oldVal.subtract(new BigDecimal("500.00")));

        System.out.println("Final balance: " + cache.get("SAV2001"));
    }
}
```

```text
main loading from DB: SAV2001
Final balance: 75500.00
```

### What are the common mistakes when using HashMap in concurrent code?

The most common mistake is assuming that read-only access is safe. Even if only one thread writes, readers without a happens-before relationship may see stale or partially constructed values.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class CommonMistakesDemo {
    // WRONG: "only one writer, so it's safe" is FALSE
    private static final Map<String, BigDecimal> cache = new HashMap<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Writer thread
        new Thread(() -> {
            cache.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("100000.00"));
        }, "Writer").start();

        // Reader thread: may NEVER see the write due to no happens-before
        new Thread(() -> {
            while (cache.get("SAV2001") == null) {
                // JIT may hoist this check out of the loop
                // Thread spins forever seeing null
            }
            System.out.println("Balance: " + cache.get("SAV2001"));
        }, "Reader").start();
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class CorrectPatternDemo {
    // CORRECT: ConcurrentHashMap provides visibility guarantees
    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> cache =
            new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Thread(() -> {
            cache.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("100000.00"));
        }, "Writer").start();

        new Thread(() -> {
            BigDecimal balance;
            while ((balance = cache.get("SAV2001")) == null) {
                Thread.onSpinWait();
            }
            System.out.println("Balance: " + balance);
        }, "Reader").start();
    }
}
```

### What about production caching with TTL and eviction?

For production caches that need time-to-live expiration, size-based eviction, and cache statistics, use a dedicated caching library built on top of ConcurrentHashMap.

```java
