// Cache<String, BigDecimal> balanceCache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
//         .maximumSize(10_000)
//         .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(5))
//         .recordStats()
//         .build();
// balanceCache.get("SAV2001", key -> loadFromDB(key));
// Thread-safe + eviction + TTL + atomic loading + metrics
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Why does ConcurrentHashMap not allow null keys or values? Because `get()` returning null would be ambiguous: it could mean the key is absent or the value is null, and there is no way to distinguish without locking.
- Can you iterate a ConcurrentHashMap safely? Yes, iterators are weakly consistent: they reflect the state at some point during or after construction but will not throw ConcurrentModificationException.
- What is the default concurrency level? In Java 8 and later, the internal design uses per-bucket locking so the old concurrency-level hint is ignored. The constructor parameter exists only for backward compatibility.
